      "id": 1,
      "text": "West Conshohocken became an official borough on October 6, 1874, taking land almost equally from Upper and Lower Merion Townships.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://westconsho.com/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before adopting its current name, the area was known as Balligomingo, a name that echoes its Native American roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Conshohocken%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Conshohocken Free Library opened in 1910, housed in the former Lukens Home donated by the Lukens family.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.conshohockenhistoricalsociety.org/chronological-timeline",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Lee Tire Plant at Spring Mill, built in the 1920s, earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places in 1984.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.conshohockenhistoricalsociety.org/chronological-timeline",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Volunteer firefighters first organized in West Conshohocken with the founding of Fire Company Number 2 in 1907.",
